---
import Growth from '../../layouts/Growth.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/growth/Hero.astro';
import AdviceCta from '../../components/growth/AdviceCta.astro';
import { leadResponse, postSchema } from '../../data/advice-wave-b';
const post = leadResponse;
---

<Growth
  title={post.title}
  description={post.description}
  schema={postSchema(post)}
  robots="index, follow"
>
  <Hero eyebrow={post.eyebrow} title={post.h1} intro={post.lede} />
  <article class="section wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <a class="back-link" href="/advice">
        ← Advice
      </a>
      <h2>What “speed-to-lead” means here</h2>
      <p>
        Speed-to-lead is the time from a form, a call, or a chat to a human
        reply that can book the job. The clock starts when the inquiry reaches
        you. It stops when a person who can put work on the calendar actually
        connects.
      </p>
      <p>
        An auto-text that says “we got it” is not that reply. It can hold the
        thread. It cannot book the job.
      </p>
      <p>
        Count nights and weekends the same as Tuesday at noon. If the phone
        rings after hours and nobody who can book answers, that minute still
        counts. The homeowner does not pause their problem until your office
        opens.
      </p>
      <h2>Why more ad spend won’t fix a slow phone</h2>
      <p>
        You already paid for the attention. Delay hands that person to the next
        company that picks up. Another ad dollar does not call them back.
      </p>
      <p>
        Missed calls and hour-later callbacks show up as “bad leads” in the
        report when they were late replies. The form was real. The job went to
        whoever answered.
      </p>
      <p>
        Measure booked jobs, not form fills. A full inbox and a quiet calendar
        usually means the phone was slow. Buy more clicks only after the replies
        are holding.
      </p>
      <h2>Targets that actually change the calendar</h2>
      <p>
        Aim to answer or return the call in minutes, not “same day.” Same-day is
        a long gap when the next company is already on the way.
      </p>
      <p>
        After hours, a covered line beats a voicemail box that gets checked at
        8am. The job that needed you tonight is often booked by morning.
      </p>
      <p>
        One owner on a truck needs a backup—person, service, or shared queue—not
        hope. If you are on a job, someone else has to be able to book the next
        one.
      </p>
      <h2>A simple weekly scoreboard</h2>
      <p>Review these four numbers before you raise the budget.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Missed calls</li>
        <li>Median callback time</li>
        <li>Booked appointments from inbound</li>
        <li>No-shows (separate problem; don’t mix with speed)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Missed calls and callback time show whether a person got there in
        minutes. Booked appointments show whether that speed became work.
        No-shows stay on the board so you can see them, and off the speed line
        so they do not excuse a late reply.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a plain check of the gap between a new inquiry and a useful reply,
        use the free{' '}
        <a href="/tools/lead-response">lead response scorecard</a>. It does not
        replace the weekly numbers.
      </p>
    </div>
    <AdviceCta />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Fixes that usually pay before new campaigns</h2>
      <p>Do these before you fund another campaign.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Call routing that rings a live person, including when the owner is
          already on a job.
        </li>
        <li>
          A missed-call text within a minute that offers two bookable times.
          That text has to lead to a person who can book, not a note that only
          says you saw it.
        </li>
        <li>
          A calendar link only after a human qualifies the job. The link is
          optional, and it does not belong in the first auto-reply.
        </li>
        <li>
          Stop judging channels until response time is stable. A slow phone
          makes every source look like a bad lead.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Intake, or the front desk in plain language, is who answers, how fast,
        and whether they can book. Get that stable. Then decide if you still
        need more traffic.
      </p>
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {post.faq.map((item) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{item.q}</summary>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        <a href="/advice">Advice</a>·
        <a href="/advice/after-a-bad-agency">
          What to ask after a marketing agency burned you
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
  <AdviceCta headline={post.ctaHeadline} body={post.ctaBody} />
</Growth>
